===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|Norwey}}, {{m|enm|Norweye}}, {{m|enm|Northwey}}, {{m|enm|Norweȝe}}, {{m|enm|Norewæiȝ}}, {{m|enm|Norwæge}}, from {{inh|en|ang|Norþweġ}}, a calque of {{der|en|non|Norðvegr|t=north way}}, from {{m|non|norðr||north}} + {{m|non|vegr||way}}, contrasted with {{m|non|suðrvegr||south way}}, i.e. [[w:Germany|Germany]], and {{m|non|austrvegr||east way}}, the [[w:Baltic states|Baltic]] lands. Norwegian (1607) is from {{cog|ML.|Norvegia}}, with the -w- from {{m|en|Norway}}. Equivalent to ''[[North]]'' + ''[[way]]''.

